# 40. Development methodology

Use TDD where practical.

Preferred cycle:

```text
requirement
↓
contract
↓
test
↓
implementation
↓
integration test
↓
evaluation
```

For deterministic functionality:

```text
write test first
```

For LLM functionality:

```text
define output schema
↓
define evaluation criteria
↓
implement prompt/agent
↓
run evaluation
↓
iterate
```

---

# 41. AI coding agent instructions

The coding agent MUST behave as an engineering agent, not as a code autocomplete system.

Before implementing a significant feature:

1. inspect the repository;
2. inspect existing architecture;
3. inspect relevant tests;
4. inspect current dependency versions;
5. inspect current official documentation if an external framework API is involved;
6. propose the smallest coherent implementation;
7. implement;
8. run tests;
9. fix failures;
10. update documentation.

Do not rewrite working code without a reason.

Do not introduce abstractions without a use case.

Do not add dependencies speculatively.

---

# 42. Documentation lookup rule

For LangChain/LangGraph/LangSmith APIs:

**official current documentation is authoritative.**

Before using an unfamiliar API:

```text
search/read current documentation
→ verify API
→ implement
```

Do not rely on model memory for rapidly changing APIs.

The LangChain ecosystem changes quickly.

---

# 43. MCP development rule

Prefer official/current MCP interfaces and current LangChain MCP integrations.

MCP should be treated as an integration boundary, not as a replacement for domain services.

The domain layer must remain usable without MCP.

---

# 44. Evaluation quality rule

Never optimize an agent solely for:

```text
"LLM said something plausible"
```

Evaluate:

```text
correctness
relevance
groundedness
completeness
traceability
tool correctness
structured output validity
```

Whenever possible compare agent output against deterministic ground truth.

---

# 45. Demo scenario

The project MUST have one complete end-to-end demo.

Example:

```text
Input:
https://github.com/example/example-repository
```

Then:

```text
1. onboard repository
2. discover repository
3. generate improvement candidate
4. human approval
5. generate requirements
6. human approval
7. generate QA plan
8. human approval
9. architecture analysis
10. human approval
11. security analysis
12. human approval
13. implementation plan
14. human approval
15. implementation in isolated workspace
16. run tests
17. QA verification
18. human approval
19. create GitHub PR
```

The demo should be reproducible.

---

# 46. Definition of Done

The project is NOT complete when the code merely runs.

It is complete when:

* [ ] LangGraph graph executes end-to-end.
* [ ] State machine is deterministic.
* [ ] State invariants are tested.
* [ ] Human approval is durable.
* [ ] Agent ownership is enforced.
* [ ] Repository discovery works.
* [ ] Repository RAG works.
* [ ] GitHub integration works.
* [ ] MCP tools work.
* [ ] Local Ollama model can be used.
* [ ] Cloud model can be used.
* [ ] LangSmith tracing works.
* [ ] Evaluation dataset exists.
* [ ] Promptfoo and/or DeepEval evaluation works.
* [ ] pytest suite passes.
* [ ] FastAPI works.
* [ ] CLI works.
* [ ] Docker Compose works.
* [ ] PostgreSQL works.
* [ ] pgvector works.
* [ ] retries are bounded.
* [ ] operations are idempotent.
* [ ] secrets are protected.
* [ ] README is complete.
* [ ] architecture documentation matches implementation.
* [ ] an end-to-end demo can produce a traceable PR.

---

# 47. Implementation order

Do NOT attempt to build everything simultaneously.

Implement in vertical slices.

## Phase 1 — foundation

Build:

```text
Python
uv
Pydantic
domain models
state machine
pytest
```

No LLM yet.

---

## Phase 2 — LangGraph

Build:

```text
AgentState
graph
routing
nodes
checkpointing
human approval
```

Use mocked agents.

---

## Phase 3 — first real agent

Implement:

```text
Repository Discovery Agent
```

Use a real LLM and structured output.

---

## Phase 4 — tools + MCP

Implement:

```text
GitHub tools
filesystem tools
repository tools
test runner
```

Expose appropriate tools through MCP.

---

## Phase 5 — RAG

Add:

```text
repository indexing
embeddings
pgvector
retrieval
```

Integrate retrieval into repository-aware agents.

---

## Phase 6 — remaining agents

Implement:

```text
PO
QA
ARCH
SEC
DEV PLAN
DEV IMPLEMENT
QA VERIFY
PR
```

One responsibility at a time.

---

## Phase 7 — LangSmith

Add:

```text
tracing
metadata
datasets
runs
evaluation hooks
```

---

## Phase 8 — evaluation

Add:

```text
golden datasets
Promptfoo
DeepEval
regression evaluation
```

---

## Phase 9 — API + CLI

Add:

```text
FastAPI
CLI
```

Both must use the same application services.

---

## Phase 10 — production packaging

Add:

```text
Docker
docker-compose
documentation
security hardening
health checks
```

---

# 48. What NOT to do

Do not:

* build a chatbot;
* create one giant agent;
* let the LLM directly manipulate database state;
* let the LLM bypass state transitions;
* hard-code one model provider;
* hard-code GitHub repository names;
* store business state only in prompts;
* rely only on vector search;
* call real APIs from unit tests;
* commit secrets;
* use deprecated LangChain APIs;
* copy old LangGraph tutorials blindly;
* add Kubernetes;
* add Kafka;
* add microservices;
* add a frontend;
* add unnecessary cloud infrastructure.

The first version should remain understandable by one senior engineer.

---

# 49. Primary engineering principle

The goal is not:

> "Build an impressive AI demo."

The goal is:

> "Build a deterministic software system in which LLM agents perform bounded reasoning inside explicit contracts, state machines, tools, evaluation loops and human approval gates."

The architecture should make it obvious where:

```text
deterministic software
```

ends and:

```text
probabilistic AI
```

begins.

That boundary is a core feature of the system.

---

# 50. First task for the coding agent

When starting from an empty repository:

DO NOT immediately write all application code.

First:

1. inspect the environment;
2. initialize the Python/uv project;
3. create the architecture skeleton;
4. create domain models;
5. implement the state machine;
6. write comprehensive state-machine tests;
7. run the tests;
8. only then begin LangGraph integration.

At the end of Phase 1 the project should already have meaningful, passing tests even though no LLM is involved.

Then continue phase by phase.

Never skip directly to the final architecture just because the coding agent can generate it.
